  faqDishImage: {fileID: 0}
  faqEntries:
  - type: 1
    text: Do you know how is goto different from lugaw and arroz caldo?
    answer: 'They are three members of the same family. Lugaw is plain rice porridge.
      Arroz caldo adds chicken, ginger, and sometimes saffron. Goto adds beef tripe.
      Different proteins, the same comforting technique. '
  - type: 1
    text: Do you know why is goto considered a comfort food?
    answer: 'Rice porridge has been a food of healing in Chinese tradition for centuries.
      Filipinos adopted it fully and expanded its role, serving goto to the sick,
      the pregnant, and anyone who needs a bowl of warmth and care. '
  - type: 1
    text: "Do you know why does \u201Carroz caldo\u201D have a Spanish name if it
      is a Chinese dish?"
    answer: 'Spanish friars and officials gave a Spanish name to a Chinese technique
      when they encountered it in Philippine kitchens. It is a mirror image of what
      happened with adobo, where a Spanish name was given to a native Filipino technique.
      Both patterns are common in Philippine culinary history. '
